<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" class="h-full">
  <head>
    <title><%= content_for(:title) || "Sourcebox" %></title>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">

    <%# Required. Both the GraphQL client and the Google login form read this token. %>
    <%= csrf_meta_tags %>
    <%= csp_meta_tag %>

    <%= yield :head %>
    <%= inertia_headers %>

    <%#
      Preconnect to the asset origin.

      In production the frontend is a separate container on a different origin, so the
      browser would otherwise pay DNS + TCP + TLS before it can even start downloading the
      JS. This overlaps that handshake with HTML parsing.
    %>
    <% if (asset_origin = FrontendAssets::Manifest.instance.asset_host).present? %>
      <link rel="preconnect" href="<%= asset_origin %>" crossorigin>
    <% end %>

    <%#
      All frontend tags come from the Vite manifest published by the frontend project.
      Rails never builds or bundles anything: see FrontendAssets::Manifest.
    %>
    <%= frontend_assets_tags %>
  </head>

  <%#
    No colour classes here on purpose. `application.css`'s own `body { @apply
    bg-background text-foreground; }` already answers this, from the same design tokens
    every component reads -- and it does so correctly for a dark mode this project
    toggles by *class*, on demand, from the cube in the nav rail. A hardcoded
    `dark:bg-slate-950` here answers a different question, "what does the OS prefer",
    using Tailwind's default media-query dark variant rather than this project's
    `@custom-variant dark (&:where(.dark, .dark *))` override -- and the two disagreeing
    is exactly how a panel ends up light-mode grey text on a dark-mode background: the
    class the user turned on took effect everywhere React renders, and this element,
    styled before any of that runs, did not move with it.
  %>
  <body class="h-full antialiased">
    <%= yield %>
  </body>
</html>
